From: SORABBYNGO Engineering <kernel@sorabbyngo.com>
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] stackprotector: per-task canary reseeding — OpenBSD-inspired

OpenBSD reseed the stack canary on every exec(2) and at context switch
boundaries (via a per-CPU canary updated in the interrupt handler).
Linux uses a single global canary. This patch:

  1. Adds a per-task canary stored in task_struct, initialised from
     get_random_long() at fork() time.

  2. On context switch (switch_to), loads the incoming task's canary
     into the __stack_chk_guard memory location used by GCC's
     -fstack-protector-strong.

  3. On exec(), reseeds the new task's canary from fresh entropy.

This means a canary leak in one process cannot be used to attack a
different process — matching OpenBSD's per-process canary behaviour.

NodeID is mixed into the canary seed so even identical processes on
different Byngonet nodes have different canaries.
---
 arch/x86/include/asm/stackprotector.h |  28 +++++++
 kernel/fork.c                         |  10 +++
 fs/exec.c                             |   8 ++
 arch/x86/kernel/process_64.c          |  16 ++++
 4 files changed, 62 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/include/asm/stackprotector.h b/arch/x86/include/asm/stackprotector.h
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/stackprotector.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/stackprotector.h
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 #ifndef _ASM_X86_STACKPROTECTOR_H
 #define _ASM_X86_STACKPROTECTOR_H
+#include <linux/random.h>
 
+/*
+ * sorabbyngo_init_task_canary - generate a per-task stack canary.
+ *
+ * Mixes NodeID into the canary so nodes with the same binary have
+ * different canaries. This mirrors OpenBSD's per-process canary
+ * seeded from arc4random() at fork time.
+ */
+static inline unsigned long sorabbyngo_init_task_canary(void)
+{
+	unsigned long canary = get_random_long();
+	extern char nodeid_hex[65];
+	u32 nodeid_word;
+
+	/* fold first 4 bytes of NodeID into canary */
+	memcpy(&nodeid_word, nodeid_hex, sizeof(u32));
+	canary ^= (unsigned long)nodeid_word;
+	canary ^= (unsigned long)nodeid_word << 32;
+
+	/* ensure low byte is never zero (GCC canary convention) */
+	if (!(canary & 0xff))
+		canary |= 0x42;
+
+	return canary;
+}
